# Backend Conventions

Style is `rubocop-rails-omakase` adjusted to repo conventions in `.rubocop.yml` (single quotes,
keyword-aligned `end`). Run it via `npm run check` (or `bin/rubocop` directly).
Start new files with `# frozen_string_literal: true` followed by a comment explaining the class's purpose.

The codebase uses `# WHY:` comments to explain non-obvious authorization and business decisions —
follow that convention rather than restating what the code does.

## Controllers

Inertia renders name the page component by path, matching `app/javascript/pages/`:

```ruby
render inertia: 'Roi/Index', props: roi_props(organization)
# failure case
render inertia: 'Roi/Index', props: roi_props(organization, errors: organization.errors.messages),
       status: :unprocessable_entity
```

Build props in a private `*_props` method returning a hash, and pass capability flags computed from
policies so the UI can hide what the user can't do: `can_edit_metrics: policy(:roi).update?`.

[app/controllers/application_controller.rb](app/controllers/application_controller.rb) enforces
`verify_authorized` and `verify_policy_scoped` via `after_action`. Every action must either
`authorize ...` / `policy_scope ...` or explicitly `skip_policy_scope`. Strong params go in a private
`*_params` method using `params.require(...).permit(...)`.

## Multi-tenancy — the single most important rule

`Current.organization` is the tenant, `current_account` is the user (aliased as `pundit_user`).
**Always scope through Pundit, never query a model unscoped:**

```ruby
# BAD — leaks across organizations
Conversation.find(params[:id])

# GOOD
policy_scope(Conversation).find(params[:id])
authorize conversation, :update?
```

A foreign ID must produce "not found", never another org's data.

## Pundit policies

Subclass `ApplicationPolicy`; predicates end in `?`. `ApplicationPolicy` defaults everything to
amplifa-admin-only, so customer access is granted explicitly. Available helpers: `amplifa_admin?`,
`customer_account?`, `customer_admin?`, `customer_user?`, `same_current_organization?(org_id)`,
`current_organization_id`, `current_membership`.

```ruby
def show?
  return true if amplifa_admin?

  customer_account? && belongs_to_user_organization?
end

class Scope < Scope
  def resolve
    return scope.all if user&.amplifa_admin?

    if customer_account? && current_organization_id
      scope.where(organization_id: current_organization_id)
    else
      scope.none
    end
  end
end
```

Roles resolve from `Current.organization_membership`, not the account — a user with no active
membership gets no customer rights.

## Services

`app/services/` classes take keyword args in `initialize` and expose a single public `#call` that
returns a hash (or raises). Keep helpers private and memoize derived data with `@x ||=`.

```ruby
class LeadImportAnalyzerService
  def initialize(csv_content:, email_column:, organization_id:)
  def call
    { summary: build_summary, rows: analyze_rows }
```

## Tests (Minitest)

Use `test "..." do` blocks with fixtures (`fixtures :all` is global) — no factories.
Policy tests instantiate the policy directly, set `Current.organization`, and `Current.reset` in `teardown`:

```ruby
test "show? returns false for customer viewing other org entries" do
  assert_not BlacklistPolicy.new(@customer_admin, @beta_unsubscribe).show?
end
```

Integration tests use the helpers in [test/test_helper.rb](test/test_helper.rb): `login_as(account)`,
`inertia_headers`, `inertia_props`, `assert_inertia_component`.

```ruby
get roi_path, headers: inertia_headers
assert_inertia_component 'Roi/Index'
```

Note `login_as` uses password `'password123'` for amplifa admins and `'password'` for everyone else
(this differs from the seed passwords in README.md). WebMock blocks outbound HTTP — stub LLM calls.

Run: `bin/rails test` (or a single file: `bin/rails test test/models/foo_test.rb`).
